(* Get started: Introduction, Quick start, Thinking in signals. Text is plain
   ASCII (Melange reads plain string literals byte by byte). *)

open Doc

(* ----- Introduction ----- *)

let intro_code =
  {|open Reativa
open Reativa.View.Mlx

let count = Signal.make 0

let () =
  View.mount_by_id "app"
    <button onClick=(fun _ -> Signal.update count (fun n -> n + 1))>
      ("Clicked ") (Signal.get count) (" times")
    </button>|}

let intro_code_re =
  {|open Reativa;
open Reativa.View.Mlx;

let count = Signal.make(0);

let () =
  View.mount_by_id(
    "app",
    <button onClick={_ => Signal.update(count, n => n + 1)}>
      {"Clicked "} {Signal.get(count)} {" times"}
    </button>,
  );|}

let intro () =
  [
    H2 ("what", "What reativa is");
    P
      "reativa is a UI library for OCaml and ReasonML, built on signals. You keep state in signals, derive values from them with computeds, and describe the page with JSX-like markup that turns into real DOM nodes. The page is built once; after that, a change to a signal reaches only the text, attributes and list rows that read it.";
    Code { file = "counter.mlx"; ocaml = intro_code; reason = intro_code_re };
    P
      "In this program the button is created a single time. The only part of it that depends on `count` is the `(Signal.get count)` in its label, so a click rewrites that one text node and leaves the rest of the page alone.";
    H2 ("feel", "What it is like to use");
    P
      "Because components run once, there are no render cycles to reason about, nothing to memoise and no dependency arrays to keep in sync. A `Signal.get` in your markup is the subscription, and the ppx wires it up for you; everything else is set once and left alone. Signals, props and events are ordinary OCaml types, so the compiler you already use checks your front end too, whether you write `.mlx` or ReasonML and compile with Melange or js_of_ocaml.";
    H2 ("fit", "Is it a good fit?");
    P
      "reativa suits teams that already write OCaml or ReasonML and want their front end checked by the same compiler, and interfaces that change in small, frequent ways, such as live values, forms and long lists, where updating single nodes pays off. The API is small enough to read in an afternoon: every module is described by its `.mli` file, and the whole [API reference](/docs/api) fits on one page. The signal graph is plain OCaml, so your state logic can be tested natively with `dune test`.";
    P
      "It is also a young project, and that comes with trade-offs. There are far fewer ready-made components, guides and integrations than in the React or Solid ecosystems, and it renders in the browser only, with no server-side rendering or hydration today. The ppx infers reactivity from the shape of your code, so a read hidden in a function from another module has to be wrapped by hand, as [Markup and components](/docs/learn/markup) explains. And the API still moves: 2.0 changed how components run, and [What's new in 2.0](/docs/whats-new) covers the upgrade.";
    H2 ("next", "Where to go next");
    P
      "The [Quick start](/docs/quick-start) gets a view on the page in four steps, and [Thinking in signals](/docs/thinking-in-signals) explains the one idea the rest of the library follows from. If you would rather read code, the [examples](/examples) are small, complete programs running live. Coding agents can start from [llms.txt](https://brnrdog.github.io/reativa/llms.txt), which lists a Markdown version of every topic.";
  ]

(* ----- Quick start ----- *)

let build_melange =
  {|# Melange: emit ES modules, then bundle the entry with Vite or esbuild
dune build @melange
# the entry is _build/default/<dir>/output/<dir>/app.js|}

let build_jsoo =
  {|# js_of_ocaml: one self-contained script, no bundler
dune build ./app.bc.js
# load it with <script src="app.bc.js"></script>|}

let quick_start () =
  [
    P
      "Four steps take you from an empty opam switch to a view on the page. The first two depend on the syntax picked at the top of the page; the rest is the same for OCaml and ReasonML.";
    H2 ("install", "1. Install the library");
    P
      "reativa needs OCaml 4.14 and dune 3.16 or later, and brings mlx and ppxlib along with it. Pin it from GitHub into your switch, and if you write ReasonML, install the `reason` package too so that dune can read `.re` files.";
    Setup
      { file = "shell"; lang = "shell"; ocaml = Examples.install_code; reason = Examples.install_code_re };
    H2 ("dune", "2. Set up dune");
    P
      "Markup in `.mlx` files comes from the mlx dialect, which you declare once in `dune-project`, while dune reads `.re` files out of the box. Either way the markup goes through the same ppx, `reativa.mlx_ppx`.";
    Setup
      {
        file = "dune-project";
        lang = "dune";
        ocaml = Examples.dune_project_code;
        reason = Examples.dune_project_code_re;
      };
    H2 ("backend", "3. Pick a backend");
    P
      "Melange compiles each module to an ES module that you bundle with Vite or esbuild, while js_of_ocaml links your program into one self-contained script and needs no bundler. The js_of_ocaml backend lives in a second package, `reativa-jsoo`, pinned from the same repository. Your views are the same with either; only the dune stanza changes, and [Backends](/docs/backends) goes into the differences.";
    Raw (Examples.backend_panels ());
    H2 ("first-view", "4. Write your first view");
    P
      "Mount the view into an element of your page, here a `<div id=\"app\">` in `index.html`. A static view like this one is created once and never touched again; giving it some state is the next step.";
    Code { file = "app.mlx"; ocaml = Examples.hello_code; reason = Examples.hello_code_re };
    P "Then build it with the backend you picked:";
    Plain { file = "shell"; lang = "shell"; source = build_melange };
    Plain { file = "shell"; lang = "shell"; source = build_jsoo };
    H2 ("next", "Next steps");
    P
      "Add state with [signals](/docs/learn/signals) and [computeds](/docs/learn/computeds), read [Thinking in signals](/docs/thinking-in-signals) to see why a component only runs once, or start from one of the [examples](/examples).";
  ]

(* ----- Thinking in signals ----- *)

let once_code =
  {|open Reativa
open Reativa.View.Mlx

let count = Signal.make 0

module Counter = struct
  let component () =
    print_endline "Counter runs";   (* printed once, at mount *)
    <div>
      <p>("Count: ") (Signal.get count)</p>
      <button onClick=(fun _ -> Signal.update count (fun n -> n + 1))>
        ("+1")
      </button>
    </div>
end

let () = View.mount_by_id "app" <Counter />|}

let once_code_re =
  {|open Reativa;
open Reativa.View.Mlx;

let count = Signal.make(0);

module Counter = {
  let component = () => {
    print_endline("Counter runs"); /* printed once, at mount */
    <div>
      <p> {"Count: "} {Signal.get(count)} </p>
      <button onClick={_ => Signal.update(count, n => n + 1)}>
        {"+1"}
      </button>
    </div>;
  };
};

let () = View.mount_by_id("app", <Counter />);|}

let region_code =
  {|(* A leaf: this text node is rewritten in place. *)
<p>("Signed in as ") (Signal.get user_name)</p>

(* A region: the whole branch is rebuilt when logged_in flips. *)
<View.Show
  condition=(fun () -> Signal.get logged_in)
  fallback=(<LoginForm />)>
  <Dashboard />
</View.Show>|}

let region_code_re =
  {|/* A leaf: this text node is rewritten in place. */
<p> {"Signed in as "} {Signal.get(user_name)} </p>

/* A region: the whole branch is rebuilt when logged_in flips. */
<View.Show
  condition={() => Signal.get(logged_in)}
  fallback={<LoginForm />}>
  <Dashboard />
</View.Show>|}

let thinking () =
  [
    P
      "Most UI libraries re-run a component whenever its state changes, then work out what to patch. reativa takes a different route: each component runs once, when it is mounted, and builds real DOM nodes. The parts of those nodes that read a signal subscribe to it, so a write updates exactly those parts and nothing else.";
    H2 ("runs-once", "A component runs once");
    Code { file = "counter.mlx"; ocaml = once_code; reason = once_code_re };
    P
      "However many times you click, `Counter runs` is printed once. The click writes `count`, and the only thing subscribed to it is the text node after `Count: `, so that is the only thing that changes.";
    H2 ("graph", "Signals, computeds and the DOM");
    P
      "Behind every page sits a small graph. Signals are its sources, and computeds derive values from them, recomputing lazily and only after something they read has changed. At the edges are effects: the text nodes and attributes your markup created, plus any `Effect.run` of your own.";
    Demo (Home.graph ());
    P
      "Each number above is its own text node. Pressing `count + 1` makes one signal write, one recompute and three text updates, and no other work.";
    H2 ("leaves-and-regions", "Leaves and regions");
    P
      "An update lands in one of two kinds of place. A **leaf** is a single text node or attribute, rewritten in place. A **region** is a part of the view whose shape depends on state, like the branch of a `View.Show` or the rows of a list, and it is rebuilt when that state changes.";
    Code { file = "account.mlx"; ocaml = region_code; reason = region_code_re };
    P
      "Leaves are the cheap case: an inline read in markup costs one text write. Reach for a region when the structure itself changes, and keep it small, because local DOM state inside it, such as focus or scroll position, does not survive a rebuild.";
    H2 ("consequences", "What follows from it");
    P
      "A few habits follow from this model. Read signals where you render them: a `Signal.get` in markup subscribes that node, while the same read in an event handler subscribes nothing, because handlers are not tracked. Derive values with computeds rather than with effects that write signals, which easily turns into a loop. And remember that a component body runs once, untracked, so a `Signal.get` there is read a single time; read inline in the markup instead, or wrap the part that must rebuild in `View.tracked`.";
    P
      "Since updates only go to the nodes that asked for them, their cost follows how much changed rather than how big the page is. The [Glossary](/docs/glossary) defines these terms precisely, and [Signals](/docs/learn/signals) is the place to start meeting the pieces one by one.";
  ]
